UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Week 10, Lesson 2: Writing a character analysis paragraph', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A character analysis paragraph opens with a topic sentence about a trait or role, embeds a short quotation, explains how the evidence works and links back to the character’s want or relationships.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWrite a topic sentence + embedded quote stem for one character trait.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Arguable trait; quote ready to embed with explanation to follow.\n\nCHECK\nStarting with plot summary (''First she went…'') instead of an analytical claim.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nWrite a full character analysis paragraph (120–160 words) with topic, embedded quote, explanation and link.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"A character analysis paragraph opens with a topic sentence about a trait or role, embeds a short quotation, explains how the evidence works and links back to the character’s want or relationships.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Writing","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN4-ECA-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Write a full character analysis paragraph (120–160 words) with topic, embedded quote, explanation and link. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using analysis paragraph on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Starting with plot summary (''First she went…'') instead of an analytical claim.. Correction: Strong answer: Arguable trait;. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Starting with plot summary (''First she went…'') instead of an analytical claim. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating analysis paragraph on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with analysis paragraph, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about analysis paragraph with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for analysis paragraph. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for analysis paragraph. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Arguable trait; quote ready to embed with explanation to follow","Starting with plot summary (''First she went'') instead of an analytical claim","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson"],"question":"Write a topic sentence + embedded quote stem for one character trait. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for analysis paragraph; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Careful use of topic sentence and explain why it matter with clear evidence","Starting with plot summary (''First she went'') instead of an analytical claim","A character analysis paragraph opens with a topic sentence about a trait or role, embeds a short quotation, explains","Arguable trait; quote ready to embed with explanation to follow"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with analysis paragraph?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named topic sentence and explain why it matter, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Apply analysis paragraph to new material and name the topic sentence and explain why it matter used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Starting with plot summary (''First she went'') instead of an analytical claim","Name topic sentence and explain why it matter only, without linking it to analysis paragraph"],"question":"Which response best applies analysis paragraph with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries analysis paragraph. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Starting with plot summary (''First she went'') instead of an analytical claim","Point to exact words in the sentence that show analysis paragraph and say what they do","Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event","Say the writing is good without naming any words"],"question":"Read: \"Under the pier, Sam found a locked tin and a dripping map.\" Which choice best uses analysis paragraph from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill with textual evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Arguable trait; quote ready to embed with explanation to follow","Starting with plot summary (''First she went'') instead of an analytical claim","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged","Name topic sentence and explain why it matter without linking it to any words in the text"],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about analysis paragraph?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Guess from the title alone with no textual clue","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged","Lengthen the answer with adjectives while keeping the same idea","Locate precise textual evidence, then explain how language choices shape meaning or effect"],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to analyse topic sentence and explain why it matter?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake","Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess","Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length","Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea"],"question":"Which revision actually improves meaning for analysis paragraph?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Accurate transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Retype the worked model as the final answer","Change fonts and claim the work is new","Starting with plot summary (''First she went'') instead of an analytical claim","Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about analysis paragraph"],"question":"Which response shows accurate use of analysis paragraph on fresh material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about analysis paragraph with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating analysis paragraph on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using analysis paragraph on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for analysis paragraph. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with analysis paragraph, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Starting with plot summary (''First she went…'') instead of an analytical claim.. Correction: Strong answer: Arguable trait;. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s4-eng-y7-w10-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise analysis paragraph.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use analysis paragraph with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify topic sentence and explain why it matters.","I can practise embedded quote independently on a new prompt.","I can notice link back needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"A character analysis paragraph opens with a topic sentence about a trait or role, embeds a short quotation, explains how the evidence works and links back to the character’s want or relationships.","options":["A character analysis paragraph opens with a topic sentence about a trait or role, embeds a short quotation, explains","A claim unrelated to the text","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no explanation"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about analysis paragraph with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nTopic: Maya’s caution protects her brother but delays the truth. Quote: ''Not yet—wait for the siren to fade.'' Explanation: she prioritises safety over curiosity. Link: the relationship shapes the plot pace.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite a topic sentence + embedded quote stem for one character trait.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Arguable trait; quote ready to embed with explanation to follow.","explain":"A character analysis paragraph opens with a topic sentence about a trait or role, embeds a short quotation, explains how the evidence works and links back to the character’s want or relationships.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Starting with plot summary (''First she went…'') instead of an analytical claim. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Analysis paragraph.","caption":"Explain then link.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Topic</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">arguable trait</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Quote</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">embedded short</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Topic: Maya’s caution protects her brother but delays the truth. Quote: ''Not yet—wait for the siren to fade.'' Explanatio Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Topic: Maya’s caution protects her brother but delays the truth. Quote: ''Not yet—wait for the siren to fade.'' Explanatio Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.","options":["Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does","A claim unrelated to the text","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no explanation"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about analysis paragraph with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nTopic: Maya’s caution protects her brother but delays the truth. Quote: ''Not yet—wait for the siren to fade.'' Explanation: she prioritises safety over curiosity. Link: the relationship shapes the plot pace. Think about how this word or relationship creates meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nUnderline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","explain":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Saying a choice is good without naming the actual detail does not show understanding. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Explain the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Start analytically.","caption":"Not a retell.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Summary</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">first she went</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Claim</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">caution delays truth</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison.","options":["A guess with no explanation","A claim unrelated to the text","Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about analysis paragraph with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nStarting with plot summary (''First she went…'') instead of an analytical claim. Here is a more productive model: Topic: Maya’s caution protects her brother but delays the truth. Quote: ''Not yet—wait for the siren to fade.'' Explanation: she prioritises safety over curiosity. Link: the relationship shapes the plot pace.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach would weaken your work, then write a corrected version.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Arguable trait; quote ready to embed with explanation to follow.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"A superficial change in length or appearance does not repair meaning. Starting with plot summary (''First she went…'') instead of an analytical claim. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example","A guess with no explanation","A claim unrelated to the text"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about analysis paragraph with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA character analysis paragraph opens with a topic sentence about a trait or role, embeds a short quotation, explains how the evidence works and links back to the character’s want or relationships. Now choose different people, facts or text to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake one fresh example demonstrating the skill and explain your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should meet the same purpose as the model, but contain original material.","explain":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new content. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Transfer to an independent example"}],"title":"Year 7, Week 10, Lesson 2: Writing a character analysis paragraph","unit_number":3,"unit_title":"Novel study","week":10,"week_in_unit":2,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nTopic: Maya’s caution protects her brother but delays the truth. Quote: ''Not yet—wait for the siren to fade.'' Explanation: she prioritises safety over curiosity. Link: the relationship shapes the plot pace.\n\nExplain